  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how particles or quasiparticles move in three-dimensional space and how that motion projects onto a two-dimensional surface, with magnetic field effects and topological braiding as the main theme. In the schematic view, two point-like objects sit inside a bulk volume, each surrounded by arrows indicating radial influence or field lines, while their paths can also be traced on a top surface. Another panel adds a vertical magnetic field, labeled \\(B\\), and shows trajectories that connect motion in the bulk to motion on the upper and lower surfaces, suggesting that a particle can travel between layers while curving along each surface.\n\nThe lower-left diagrams compare different exchange paths. Two colored loops link numbered positions, showing alternative ways of moving particles around one another. The transformed version on the right, with dashed gray reference loops, emphasizes that these paths can be continuously deformed yet still represent distinct braiding structures. The final panel extends this to several particles on two parallel surfaces, connected by dashed vertical lines. Their curved trajectories form a more complicated braid, indicating that exchanges can involve both in-plane motion and transfer between surfaces under the applied magnetic field.",
